# Line-ending policy for PRIMEMET.
#
# Deliberately conservative: this file declares HOW Git should treat content. A blanket `eol=lf`
# override would rewrite every already-committed file on the next checkout, producing a large
# whitespace-only diff unrelated to any real change, so the override is limited to `*.mjs` — the
# one extension whose working-tree bytes are hashed and compared across machines. See the
# MANDATORY block below.
#
# Default: let Git detect text and normalise to LF in the repository (the canonical form), while
# each developer's working tree keeps their platform convention.
#
# MEASURED (Windows, core.autocrlf=true): this line does NOT silence the
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" warning on `git add`.
# Only an explicit `eol=lf` override does.

* text=auto

# --- Explicit text types ---------------------------------------------------
# These are always text; normalise in the repository. No `eol` override, so no rewrite —
# except `*.mjs`, which is pinned to LF for the reason given in the next block.
*.ts        text
*.tsx       text
*.js        text
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.css       text
*.prisma    text
*.sql       text
*.toml      text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.example   text
*.gitignore text
.gitignore  text
.gitattributes text

# --- MANDATORY: `*.mjs` is pinned to LF in the working tree -------------------
# Migration and schema integrity digests are computed from the *working-tree* source bytes:
# `db/migrations/index.mjs` loads each migration with `readFileSync`, and `db/ledger.mjs` hashes
# that text. The digests are frozen into `_schema_migrations`, asserted by `check-adversarial`
# ("v001's identity is unchanged") and re-computed on every verify, so the same commit must hash
# identically on every platform.
#
# With only `text`, a Windows checkout under `core.autocrlf=true` writes CRLF, every `*.mjs`
# digest changes, and the consequences are not cosmetic:
#   - `check-adversarial` fails two identity assertions that are correct on LF checkouts;
#   - a database initialised from a CRLF checkout records CRLF digests, so verifying the same
#     database from an LF checkout reports drift for a migration nobody edited.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF, which makes the hash canonical by construction. It does
# NOT change any repository blob (they are already normalised to LF) and does NOT alter the
# hashed content — it only stops the checkout from rewriting it. Measured on a stock Windows
# clone: `check-adversarial` goes 130 passed / 2 failed -> 132 passed / 0 failed, with
# `git ls-files --eol` reporting `w/lf` for every `*.mjs`.
#
# Do not "simplify" this back to plain `text`: the four `*.mjs`/`*.ts` digests above would
# silently diverge between platforms again, and only on a fresh clone — never on the tree the
# change was authored in.

# --- Always binary ---------------------------------------------------------
# Never diff, never convert. Declared so Git cannot corrupt them by guessing.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.avif      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary

# --- Generated / vendored --------------------------------------------------
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
prisma/migrations/** -diff linguist-generated

